From e937e6da55ba452339a75d5a80f272e085f431cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:37:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] Let a caller name the peripheral it means

Slot 0 is the only slot that holds more than one peripheral - the keyboard,
the joystick and the speaker all live there, while slots 1 to 7 take exactly
one - and their command ids overlap. Payload size now keeps the commands
apart, but it cannot separate a query whose answer is the same width:
keyboard_query_rocker and JOY_QUERY_EXIT_EVENT are both id 2 and both answer
one byte, so a broadcast lands on whichever peripheral the slot happens to
hold first, which is static initialisation order.

peripheral_command_by_id() and peripheral_query_by_id() name the peripheral by
its namespaced id, the way peripheral_save_state_by_name() already names it by
its human name. The two kinds of string are kept apart so that neither call
has to guess which it was handed. Nothing is renumbered and Peripheral_t is
untouched, so a peripheral built against the current ABI still works; the
broadcast calls stay for everything an id uniquely identifies.

The names match the ones upstream proposes for the same job in
linappleii/linapple#359, so if that series lands this commit is a deletion
rather than a rename across its callers.

The rocker query is the one caller that needed it, in all four frontends and
in the video refresh. The joystick's exit event is left where it is with a
note: nothing sets the flag it reports and nothing asks for it, and removing a
published enum value would break a source build for no gain.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
